<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Museum of Indonesia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1778</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Museum Gajah, Museum Nasional Indonesia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, popularly called Museum Gajah, is the main archaeological and historical museum of Indonesia, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>. It grew from the collections of the Batavian Society of Arts and Sciences, founded in 1778, and holds stone inscriptions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> statues, ceramics and ethnographic objects, among them the Portuguese padrão of 1522 from the Sunda Kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-museum-of-indonesia.jpg" alt="National Museum of Indonesia" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chainwit. · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Front_facade_-_National_Museum_Indonesia,_Jakarta_(2025)_-_img_07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
